  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on a low-price, high-volume model. It offers a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products in a wide range of categories [10-K line 00134]. The company's business model leverages volume purchasing, efficient distribution, and reduced handling to operate profitably at lower gross margins than most other retailers [10-K line 00134]. It operates in over 20 countries with a total of 939 warehouses as of August 30, 2026 [release line 00021].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes globally with various types of retailers including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, and other warehouse clubs like Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190]. The company's competitive advantages include its membership format, which reinforces member loyalty and provides continuing fee revenue, and its pricing authority, which is perceived by members as consistently offering the most competitive values [10-K line 00398]. Additionally, Costco’s private-label brand, Kirkland Signature, is noted for being high quality and offered at prices generally lower than national brands [10-K line 00192].\n\n## Growth Drivers\nCostco's growth is driven by several factors including increasing net sales, particularly comparable sales, which are achieved through increased shopping frequency and average ticket size [10-K line 00398]. Opening new warehouses also contributes to net sales growth; in 2026, the company opened 28 new warehouses [release line 00014]. The company's ability to maintain high membership renewal rates and increase penetration of Executive memberships also supports profitability [10-K line 00406]. Additionally, its e-commerce operations and digitally-enabled sales are growing significantly, representing 11% of total net sales in 2026 [10-K line 00153].\n\n## Catalysts\nSeveral catalysts support Costco’s continued performance. One significant catalyst is the ongoing expansion of membership fee revenue, driven by new member sign-ups, fee increases, and upgrades to Executive memberships [10-K line 00456]. Another key catalyst is the company's strong focus on employee retention and development, which contributes to high productivity and low turnover rates [10-K line 00181]. Furthermore, the company's strategic investments in technology and digital platforms enhance member convenience and broaden product offerings [10-K line 00153].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn 2026, Costco reported a net income increase of 14% to $9.226 billion [release line 00020]. The company also opened 28 new warehouses, including three relocations, for a total of 25 net new warehouses in the U.S., Canada, and Other International segments [10-K line 00415]. Moreover, the Board of Directors approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [10-K line 00423]. The company received IEEPA tariff refunds totaling $184 million in 2026, with $174 million recorded as a reduction to merchandise costs and $10 million as interest income [10-K line 00426].\n\n## Material Risks\nCostco faces several material risks that could affect its performance. These include economic conditions, such as inflation or deflation, which can impact the cost of goods sold and member spending patterns [10-K line 00398]. Competition is fierce across global, national, and regional wholesalers and retailers [10-K line 00190]. Tariff impacts also pose a risk; while tariffs affect some merchandise costs, the degree of exposure depends on factors such as type of goods, rates imposed, and timing [10-K line 00403]. Additionally, fluctuations in currency exchange rates influence international operations' performance [10-K line 00409]. Rising costs associated with employees, including health-care costs and wages, are partially outside the company's control but are managed through its compensation philosophy [10-K line 00407].",
